{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Parallelism 2, but split output ignored — each node ran the full suite",
    "Explanation": [
      "The config sets `parallelism: 2`, and CircleCI did spin up two containers (CIRCLE_NODE_TOTAL=2, indices 0 and 1). The command does call `circleci tests glob ... | circleci tests split --split-by=name` and writes a correct per-node list to /tmp/split.txt — node 0 got src/test_goodbye.sh and node 1 got src/test_hello.sh. However, the test-execution loop iterates over the raw glob `for f in src/test_*.sh`, completely ignoring /tmp/split.txt. So each node executed both test files (Goodbye and Hello), RAN=2 while EXPECTED=1, which trips the guard and exits 1 on both containers. Thus the workload was not actually split; it was duplicated, giving no speed-up and a red build. The fix is not to change parallelism or restructure the workflow (the instruction forbids modifying the workflow anyway) but to actually consume the split output — e.g. `TESTS=$(circleci tests glob \"src/test_*.sh\" | circleci tests split --split-by=name)` and loop over $TESTS (or `xargs -n1 bash`), so each node only runs its assigned subset and RAN matches EXPECTED."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
      "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
      "  \"suite_split\": false",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
        "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
        "  \"suite_split\": false",
        "}"
      ]
    ],
