    "Explanation": [
      "The job log shows dockerize repeatedly trying to connect to localhost:5432 and getting connection refused, then timing out. That means there is no Postgres server listening on that port in the job environment. In CircleCI Docker executor jobs you must run a database as a secondary container (an image listed under the job's docker key) or run Postgres inside the primary container yourself. The provided config.yml only starts a node image; it does not start a Postgres container. The correct fix is to add a Postgres service image to the job (and set any required POSTGRES_* env vars) so a Postgres server is running and dockerize can connect. Optionally you can increase the dockerize timeout if the DB needs more time to initialize.",
      "",
      "What to change (summary):",
      "- Add a Postgres image under jobs.build.docker (a second image). CircleCI will start it as a linked service container available on localhost:5432.",
      "- Provide required environment variables for Postgres (POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_DB, POSTGRES_PASSWORD) if your app expects them.",
      "- Keep the dockerize wait step (or increase its -timeout) so the job waits until Postgres is ready.",
      "",
      "Example config change (conceptual snippet):",
      "- Under jobs.build.docker, list two images: the primary node image and a postgres image (for example postgres:13 or circleci/postgres:9.6). Provide POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_DB/POSTGRES_PASSWORD via environment. Then the existing dockerize -wait tcp://localhost:5432 -timeout 1m will succeed once Postgres is ready.",
      "",
      "Notes and troubleshooting:",
      "- If you use the official postgres image you may need to set POSTGRES_PASSWORD (it refuses to start with an empty password unless using a specific config). circleci/postgres images may have defaults, but explicitly setting env vars is safer.",
      "- If your app needs a database schema/migrations, add a step to run migrations after the DB is up.",
      "- If the DB still takes longer than 1 minute, raise -timeout (for example 2m or 5m).",
      "- If instead you prefer to run Postgres inside the node container (not recommended), you would need to install and start Postgres there; easier is to use a service container.",
      "",
      "Why this addresses the job.log: the connection refused errors happen because no process is listening at 127.0.0.1:5432. Starting a Postgres service container ensures a server listens there so dockerize can connect and the job can proceed."
    ],
